Assistant: Developing keen sensory awareness for food freshness requires understanding both the capabilities and limitations of our natural assessment tools. While our senses evolved to protect us from spoiled food, modern food systems can sometimes confuse these ancient signals. Fresh produce should feel firm and vibrant — that slight give in berries or soft spots on stone fruit often indicates cellular breakdown has begun. With seafood, trust the clean, oceanic scent; any hint of ammonia suggests protein degradation, even if the flesh still looks acceptable. Color changes in meat can be deceptive — oxidation can occur without spoilage, while dangerous bacteria can proliferate before visible signs appear. This is why combining multiple senses matters: check the texture, smell deeply, and observe the surface sheen. Storage conditions profoundly affect how accurately we can judge — foods kept at proper temperatures maintain their sensory integrity longer. When in doubt, choosing the freshest option available and consuming foods closer to their source reduces the guesswork. Building this sensory vocabulary takes practice, but it's worth developing for both safety and enjoying food at its peak.

